---
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const approaches = [
  ['Code & coding agents', 'You can build the integration directly and own its context, tests, execution, and maintenance.', 'How will API knowledge stay connected to the workflow as either one changes?'],
  ['API & service catalogs', 'You need a shared view of services, APIs, and ownership.', 'Can the system knowledge also drive workflow creation and identify the managed steps affected by a change?'],
  ['Contract checks', 'You need to detect and review incompatible API changes.', 'Can you connect a detected change to a specific workflow step and a tested replacement?'],
  ['Workflow & integration platforms', 'You need to compose systems, map data, and operate integrations.', 'Does the approach fit your internal APIs, and what work is needed to keep those definitions and workflows aligned?'],
  ['Durable execution', 'You need workflow progress, retries, and recovery across failures.', 'Who will build the drafting, API knowledge, validation, approval, and change-review process around the runtime?'],
];
---
<BaseLayout title="Compare approaches — Where Atlas fits" description="Compare the work of building and maintaining internal API integrations. Evaluate system knowledge, workflow execution, change review, coverage, and operating cost." pathname="/compare/">
<SiteHeader current="compare" />
<main id="main">
<header class="page-hero narrow">
<p class="eyebrow">Choose the right approach</p>
<h1>The code is one part.<br />The context has to keep up.</h1>
<p class="lede">Atlas is worth evaluating when understanding connected APIs and operating their workflows need to stay together. Use your own integration to decide where it adds value.</p>
<div class="actions">
<a class="button" href="/evaluate/">Evaluate a workflow <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
</a>
<a class="button secondary" href="/how-it-works/">Explore the product</a>
</div>
</header>
<section class="section narrow reference">
<header class="section-heading">
<div>
<p class="eyebrow">Different tools. Different work.</p>
<h2>Compare the responsibility,<br />not the feature count.</h2>
</div>
</header>
<table class="compare-table">
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Approach</th>
<th scope="col">The starting point</th>
<th scope="col">The question to test</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>{approaches.map(([approach,starting,question])=>
<tr>
<td>{approach}</td>
<td data-label="The starting point">{starting}</td>
<td data-label="The question to test">{question}</td>
</tr>)}</tbody>
</table>
<p class="term" style="margin-top:24px">These approaches overlap and can work together. This is an evaluation framework, not a claim that another product lacks a capability.</p>
</section>
<section class="section platform-section">
<div class="narrow">
<header class="center-heading">
<p class="eyebrow">The Atlas approach</p>
<h2>Keep the understanding<br />with the work.</h2>
<p>Start with approved operations. Use them to build a workflow. Keep the relationship visible when a connected contract changes.</p>
</header>
<div class="value-grid">
<article>
<h3>Know the inputs.</h3>
<p>Ground workflow drafts in connected API definitions and approved operations. Validate steps and mappings against those contracts.</p>
</article>
<article>
<h3>Review the execution.</h3>
<p>Test and approve the exact version that runs. Temporal provides durable execution; your team reviews retry and duplicate-handling rules.</p>
</article>
<article>
<h3>Follow the change.</h3>
<p>After contract rediscovery, identify affected managed steps. For breaking changes, pause new intake, test the replacement, and approve it before activation.</p>
</article>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section narrow split-section">
<div>
<p class="eyebrow">Already using Temporal?</p>
<h2>Keep the durability.<br />Evaluate the work around it.</h2>
</div>
<div>
<p>Atlas uses Temporal. The evaluation is whether connecting API knowledge, workflow drafting, tests, approvals, and contract-change review reduces the work your team would otherwise own.</p>
<p>Compare the complete operating process on one workflow: implementation effort, failure recovery, contract maintenance, and the cost of another service to run.</p>
<a class="text-link" href="/how-it-works/">See the Atlas lifecycle <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
</a>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section narrow split-section reference">
<div>
<p class="eyebrow">Replacing an integration platform?</p>
<h2>Prove one flow.<br />Keep a way back.</h2>
</div>
<div>
<p>For a MuleSoft or other platform migration, inventory the APIs, mappings, schedules, permissions, and side effects first. Check coverage before treating Atlas as a replacement.</p>
<p>Compare outputs in an isolated environment. Rehearse failures, define the traffic switch, and agree how in-flight work and rollback will be handled.</p>
<a class="text-link" href="/how-it-works/#mulesoft">Plan the first migration <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
</a>
</div>
</section>
<aside class="maturity narrow">
<p class="eyebrow">Project direction</p>
<div>
<h2>A maintained map for engineers and agents.</h2>
<p>The goal is to keep system knowledge useful as APIs and tools change. Today's scope is connected APIs and Atlas-managed workflows. Broader discovery and general agent access are still ideas to validate.</p>
</div>
</aside>
<section class="closing">
<p class="eyebrow">Your systems are the real test</p>
<h2>Make the comparison concrete.</h2>
<a class="button" href="/evaluate/">Build your evaluation brief <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
</a>
</section>
</main>
<SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
